issue(id="0010" status="open")
  title Stress test aggressive whitespace compaction
  user-story
    as-a maintainer of puggers
    i-want a focused stress suite for whitespace compaction
    so-that we can measure how far normalization can go before semantics or readability break
  premise.
    Several future formatter improvements will be tempted to compact whitespace:
    collapsing redundant spaces, flattening indentation-adjacent text, trimming
    blank lines, or minimizing raw-text payloads. In Pug, those moves can be
    deceptively dangerous because whitespace carries meaning in piped text,
    dotted blocks, inline tag adjacency, and interpolation-heavy prose. A
    dedicated stress test task should push compaction hard on representative and
    adversarial fixtures so we can identify the exact boundaries where
    normalization stops being safe.
  acceptance-criteria
    criterion Add a fixture suite that exercises aggressive whitespace compaction across inline text, piped text, dotted blocks, raw blocks, comments, and interpolation-heavy examples.
    criterion Record which compaction transforms are safe, conditionally safe, or clearly unsafe, with examples for each category.
    criterion Include cases where compaction changes rendered output, not just source appearance, so regressions are obvious.
    criterion Produce a short summary of compaction findings that later formatting tasks can treat as policy input rather than rediscovering ad hoc.
  review-items
    item(status="open") Decide whether compaction testing should include HTML-imported fixtures, hand-authored Pug fixtures, or both.
    item(status="open") Confirm whether blank-line compaction belongs in the same policy bucket as inline space compaction or should be evaluated separately.
    item(status="open") Make sure examples cover both “safe to compact” cases and “must preserve exactly” cases so the suite does not become one-sided.

